      "prompt": "You translate ordinary questions into exactly one read-only DuckDB SELECT or WITH query.\n\nReturn SQL only. Never write files, install software, calculate values yourself, or invent columns.\n\nUse only the supplied schema. Preserve relevant scope from earlier turns unless the newest question changes it.\n\nA requested rate must use its stated numerator and denominator. Percentage-point change means later percentage minus earlier percentage, not relative growth.\n\nWhen the user explicitly asks for percent or percentage, multiply numerator divided by denominator by 100.\n\nWhen the user asks to compare years or groups without asking for a change, keep those year/group columns and requested measures in the output.\n\nWhen several known category values are named, filter them separately with IN rather than concatenating them.\n\nUse NULLIF for denominators. Missing values remain missing and are not zero.\n\nSCHEMA:\nCREATE TABLE \"lpf_outreach\" (\n  \"School Name\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Block\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Students Reached\" BIGINT,\n  \"Applications Expected\" BIGINT,\n  \"Contact Teacher\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Date\" VARCHAR\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE \"lpf_applications\" (\n  \"Applicant\" VARCHAR,\n  \"School\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Course\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Status\" VARCHAR,\n  \"Marks\" BIGINT\n);\n\nKNOWN CATEGORICAL VALUES (local or trusted 27B tier only):\n{\"lpf_outreach\": {\"Block\": [\"Atri\", \"Barachatti\", \"Belaganj\", \"Dobhi\", \"Fatehpur\", \"Wazirganj\"]}, \"lpf_applications\": {\"Course\": [\"Beautician\", \"Computer Operator\", \"Diploma Civil Engineering\", \"ITI Electrician\", \"Nursing Assistant\", \"Retail Sales Associate\", \"Tailoring & Fashion Design\"], \"Status\": [\"Incomplete\", \"Rejected\", \"Submitted\", \"Verified\"]}}\n\nCURRENT QUESTION:\nschool wise application count matching names approximately even with typos or short forms, zero counted for schools with no applications, alphabetical by school (each application should count towards only its single best matching school)",
      "response": "```sql\nWITH \n-- 1. Extract distinct schools from the outreach table\noutreach_schools AS (\n    SELECT DISTINCT \"School Name\" AS school_name\n    FROM lpf_outreach\n),\n-- 2. Prepare applications for matching\napps AS (\n    SELECT \n        \"School\" AS app_school,\n        \"Applicant\"\n    FROM lpf_applications\n),\n-- 3. Calculate similarity scores between each application school and each outreach school\n-- We use a simple Levenshtein distance approximation or exact match logic.\n-- Since DuckDB doesn't have a built-in fuzzy match that returns a single \"best\" easily without window functions,\n-- we will compute the distance for all pairs, then pick the minimum distance per application.\n-- Note: DuckDB has `levenshtein` function.\nmatches AS (\n    SELECT \n        a.app_school,\n        o.school_name,\n        levenshtein(lower(a.app_school), lower(o.school_name)) AS dist\n    FROM apps a\n    CROSS JOIN outreach_schools o\n),\n-- 4. Find the best match for each application\nbest_matches AS (\n    SELECT \n        app_school,\n        school_name,\n        dist,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY app_school ORDER BY dist ASC) AS rn\n    FROM matches\n),\n-- 5. Filter to only the best match (rn = 1)\n-- We might want to filter out matches that are too far apart, but the prompt says \"matching names approximately\", \n-- implying we should try to match them. If no match is close, it might be null, but the prompt asks for \"zero counted for schools with no applications\".\n-- This implies we should count applications that *do* match. \n-- However, \"each application should count towards only its single best matching school\" suggests we assign every application to its closest school.\n-- If the closest school is very far, is it a match? Usually, in these tasks, we assume the data is reasonably clean or we just take the closest.\n-- Let's assume we take the closest one.\nfinal_matches AS (\n    SELECT \n        app_school,\n        school_name\n    FROM best_matches\n    WHERE rn = 1\n)\n-- 6. Aggregate counts per school\nSELECT \n    o.school_name AS \"School Name\",\n    COUNT(f.app_school) AS \"Application Count\"\nFROM outreach_schools o\nLEFT JOIN final_matches f ON o.school_name = f.school_name\nGROUP BY o.school_name\nORDER BY o.school_name ASC;\n```",
